WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.167
SQL commands are often grouped by the type of work they perform.

00:00:04.167 --> 00:00:12.152
DDL defines structure. Commands such as CREATE, ALTER, and DROP change the database schema.

00:00:12.152 --> 00:00:15.227
You saw much more of this in Module 2.

00:00:15.227 --> 00:00:19.082
DQL is commonly used to describe reading data, especially with SELECT.

00:00:19.082 --> 00:00:25.612
DML changes stored rows. INSERT adds rows, UPDATE changes existing rows, DELETE removes rows,

00:00:25.612 --> 00:00:29.757
and newer PostgreSQL versions also support MERGE for synchronization patterns.

00:00:29.757 --> 00:00:37.472
Transaction-control commands decide whether a group of changes should be kept or undone.

00:00:37.472 --> 00:00:45.317
The labels are useful, but the practical idea is simpler: know whether your statement is only reading data,

00:00:45.317 --> 00:00:49.457
changing rows, changing structure, or controlling a transaction.

00:00:49.457 --> 00:00:53.102
That tells you how carefully you need to verify the result.

00:00:53.102 --> 00:00:58.627
When reading the example, do not read punctuation one character at a time.

00:00:58.627 --> 00:01:06.237
Start with the purpose of the query, then identify the input rows, the operation being applied,

00:01:06.237 --> 00:01:08.152
and the shape of the expected output.

00:01:08.152 --> 00:01:14.722
In a real setting such as an application safely inserting or updating business records,

00:01:14.722 --> 00:01:22.297
this habit makes code reviews much easier because you can explain what the query is supposed to guarantee

00:01:22.297 --> 00:01:24.552
before discussing syntax.

00:01:24.552 --> 00:01:30.937
After running it, inspect a few records manually and check that the result matches that explanation.
